Saberhagen's "Swords" books have a collection of artifacts that seem 
like they might be interesting additions to NetHack.  The Twelve are:

1) Wayfinder -- The Sword of Wisdom
   
   In the books: Whoever wields Wayfinder will eventually find all he 
   needs to accomplish a specific goal; the sword directs him.

   In the game: The Sword might be directed to look for a certain 
   artifact, which would then be guaranteed to exist somewhere in the 
   dungeon, and it would lead the player in the right direction.  It 
   might be 20 levels down, but that's another issue.

2) Coinspinner -- The Sword of Chance

   In the books: superluck, but the sword refuses to stay with one owner
   long.  

   In the game: luck -> 10, but a small chance of vanishing each rest 
   period.

3) Woundhealer -- The Sword of Mercy

   In the books: heals.  Really ... 

   In the game: Perhaps 1-3 hps back per application, a superregeneration
   ring.  Same thing to monsters who are attacked with it.  Must be wielded
   for effects.

4) Townsaver -- The Sword of Fury

   In the books: would slay any opponent, but only when the wielder is guarding
   a fortification containing unarmed humans.
  
   In the game: Well, it's still a real neat sword *grin*.

5) Sightblinder -- The Sword of Stealth
  
   In the books, the ability to see past all disguises to the true nature of 
   a being, and also one appears to others as someone they love or fear, 
   occasionally switching forms.

   In the game: Stealth, See Invisible, Stethoscope as Healer, and low INT 
   monsters automatically friendly.  High INT ones may notice the deception.
   This is nicely balanced since everyone hates friendly monsters ...

6) Doomgiver -- The Sword of Justice

   In the books: Would do to an opponent whatever it tried to do to the 
   wielder, and *first*. 

   In the game: Ridiculously powerful.  Perhaps get rid of the *first* 
   part.

7) Mindsword -- The Sword of Rule (?)

   In the books: Could be wielded to subjugate any who saw the blade.
   
   In the game: Again, possibly too powerful.  A mega-taming when drawn?

8) Soulcutter -- The Tyrant's Blade

   In the books: removed volition from all in the area.

   In the game: Paralyze everything on the level, including the player.  
   If he's lucky, he can resheathe it before starving.

9) Dragonslicer -- The Sword of Heros

   In the books: Cut Dragonarmor like butter.
 
   In the game: Goes against dragons as if their armor class is 10. 

10) Farslayer -- The Sword of Vengeance

    In the books: With the proper incantation, Farslayer would fly to 
    the heart of a being named and bury itself there.

    In the game: Kill named monsters on the level.  Pray that a monster 
    doesn't pick up the sword before you get to it!

11) Stonecutter -- The Sword of Siege

    In the books: Cut stone perfectly, easily, and accurately.

    In the game: Megapickaxe.  Drill your way anywhere not magically guarded,
    even into shops.

12) Shieldbreaker -- The Sword of Force
 
    In the books: Anyone going against it with a weapon would have the 
    weapon shattered and would die.  Unarmed people had an advantage, 
    becuase in such a combat Shieldbreaker would not do them harm, nor 
    allow its wielder to let go.

    In the game: Similar.  Useful for destroying armed creatures, but 
    you don't get their weapons, and *don't* use it against an unarmed 
    helpless poor l'il shopkeeper!

Notes:
   The swords are all probably base +2 longswords.  When in use, they 
make a thudding noise.  Also, there is apparently a very small chance of 
polymorph with each use of Woundhealer, though Saberhagen hasn't answered
this yet, quite ...

